const title = 'Foods That Cause Breakouts: My Hidden Skin Triggers';
const description = 'Foods that may cause breakouts, plus stress, alcohol, dairy and sleep: what the research shows about sugar, dairy and alcohol and your skin, and how I find my own triggers.';
const faq = [
  ['What foods cause breakouts?', 'The foods most often linked with acne are high glycemic foods such as sugary drinks, sweets and refined pastries, and for some people dairy. Highly processed foods are also commonly suspected. The evidence is strongest for glycemic load, weaker for dairy, and triggers differ a lot from person to person.'],
  ['Does sugar cause acne?', 'Not on its own, but frequent blood sugar spikes may raise insulin and androgen activity, which can increase oil production and inflammation. A systematic review of randomized trials found that lower glycemic load diets reduced acne severity, though the number of trials was small. An occasional treat is unlikely to be the problem.'],
  ['Does dairy cause acne?', 'A meta-analysis of 14 observational studies (78,529 people aged 7 to 30) found higher odds of acne with any dairy, with milk, yogurt and cheese all linked, but the authors urged caution because of bias and differences between studies. Many people tolerate dairy fine, so I would test one change at a time.'],
  ['Does alcohol affect skin?', 'It can dehydrate the skin, widen blood vessels and disturb sleep, which may show up as dullness, puffiness and redness. In a large study of US women, white wine and liquor were linked with a higher risk of rosacea, while beer and red wine were not significantly linked.'],
  ['Can lack of sleep cause breakouts?', 'Poor sleep can raise stress hormones and inflammation and slow skin repair, which may make breakouts and dullness more likely. Sleep is not the only factor, but I treat it as part of skincare.'],
  ['How do I find my own food triggers?', 'I would look for patterns rather than villains: note when skin flares, change one thing at a time for several weeks, and keep eating enough. If you are thinking about cutting a whole food group, a dermatologist or registered dietitian can help you do it safely.'],
];
