<section class="p art-top"><a class="up arrow" href="/notes/" style="color:#8A8A8A">Back to notes</a>
<div class="art-head"><div><div class="up" style="color:#8A8A8A">Notes · Skin concerns · Gut and skin, part 3 · Updated October 2026</div><h1 class="hd">Foods That Cause Breakouts: My Hidden Skin Triggers</h1><p class="serif stand">Sometimes skin inflammation is not about skincare at all. Food, stress, sleep, alcohol and hormones can all influence how your skin behaves. This is part 3 of my skin barrier and gut health series: the hidden triggers I look at when my skin is acting up, and how strong the evidence really is for each.</p><div class="hand" style="transform:rotate(-2deg);font-size:34px;margin-top:10px">it is not always the cream</div></div>
<img class="art-hero" src="/img/bakery-hero.webp" alt="Pastries on a white plate at an outdoor cafe table, a hand holding a berry pastry and an iced matcha latte" style="width:100%;max-width:1240px;height:auto;aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:cover;display:block;margin:0 auto"></div></section>
<section class="p art-grid"><div class="art-body">

<p>When my skin gets reactive, the first thought is usually “which product did I use?”. A better question is also <strong>what else is going on in my body?</strong> Food, stress, sleep and hormones can all change how skin behaves, and skincare cannot fully make up for them.</p>
<p>This note covers the triggers mentioned most often: high glycemic foods, highly processed foods, chronic stress and skipped meals, alcohol, dairy and poor sleep. For each one I explain what may happen in the body, what it can do to the skin, and what the research can and cannot say. Skin responses are very individual. This is general information from my own reading, not medical advice.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>The evidence is strongest for high glycemic foods and acne, and weaker, but real, for dairy.</li>
<li>Frequent blood sugar spikes matter more than an occasional treat.</li>
<li>Stress, skipped meals and too little sleep raise stress hormones and slow barrier repair.</li>
<li>Alcohol is a common rosacea trigger, and the type of drink seems to matter.</li>
<li>Look for patterns in your own skin and change one thing at a time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How strong is the evidence for each trigger?</h2>
<p>Here is how I sort what I read. “Stronger” still does not mean certain.</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Trigger</th><th>What the research suggests</th><th>My takeaway</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">High glycemic foods</em></td><td>Randomized trials suggest lower glycemic load diets reduce acne severity</td><td>Limit frequent spikes</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Dairy</em></td><td>Observational studies link it with more acne, with caveats</td><td>Test it, do not assume</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Alcohol</em></td><td>Linked with rosacea risk, especially white wine and liquor</td><td>Notice your own flushing</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Stress and sleep</em></td><td>Stress hormones impair the skin barrier</td><td>Treat as part of skincare</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Processed foods</em></td><td>Often suspected, evidence is less direct</td><td>Keep occasional</td></tr></table>

<h2>High glycemic foods</h2>
<p>This group includes white sugar, candy, sweet drinks and refined pastries. As I understand it, this is what may happen:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>blood sugar rises quickly, followed by an insulin spike</li>
<li>insulin can increase androgen hormones</li>
<li>androgens stimulate the sebaceous glands</li>
<li>more oil plus inflammation raises the risk of acne</li>
</ul>
<p>A systematic review and meta-analysis pooled three randomized trials and found that lower glycemic load diets reduced acne severity compared with control diets, and observational studies linked higher glycemic load with more severe acne. The authors also stress that the number of trials is small and better ones are needed.</p>
<blockquote class="quote">The issue is frequent spikes, not occasional treats.</blockquote>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/img/donut-display.webp" alt="A bakery display of glazed, matcha, pink and chocolate crunch donuts and bagels" style="max-height:none;object-fit:contain"><figcaption>A donut display at a bakery.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Highly processed foods</h2>
<p>Think fast food, ultra-processed snacks and processed meats. They tend to be high in omega-6 fats, refined starch and salt and low in fibre and antioxidants. That may:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>promote pro-inflammatory pathways</li>
<li>increase oxidative stress, which can damage skin cells</li>
<li>speed up collagen breakdown</li>
</ul>
<p>The link from these foods to specific skin outcomes is less direct than for glycemic load, so I treat this one as a general “do not make it the base” rule. Dull skin, congestion and slower healing are what I would watch for. I explain what I eat instead in <a href="/notes/foods-and-supplements-for-skin/">my food and supplements guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Chronic stress and skipping meals</h2>
<p>Irregular eating and constant stress put the body under pressure. The chain I keep in mind is:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>irregular eating and stress can raise cortisol</li>
<li>cortisol increases inflammation</li>
<li>unstable blood sugar stresses the body</li>
<li>skin barrier repair slows down</li>
</ul>
<p>A review of 21 studies on stress and the epidermal barrier describes how psychological stress activates the body’s stress-hormone system and impairs barrier function: fewer barrier lipids and proteins, lower hydration and more water loss through the skin. On skin, that can mean breakouts, sensitivity and slower wound healing. Regular, warm meals are one of the simplest ways I support my skin when life is busy.</p>
<div class="figrow"><figure class="fig"><img src="/img/green-wrap.webp" alt="Green wraps filled with salmon, cucumber and greens on a green plate" style="height:auto;aspect-ratio:4/5;object-fit:cover"><figcaption>A wrap with salmon, cucumber and greens.</figcaption></figure><figure class="fig"><img src="/img/green-smoothie-pour.webp" alt="Pouring a green smoothie from a blender bottle into a glass" style="height:auto;aspect-ratio:4/5;object-fit:cover"><figcaption>Pouring a green smoothie.</figcaption></figure></div>

<h2>Alcohol</h2>
<p>Alcohol is often a hidden trigger, especially for sensitive or redness-prone skin. It may:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>dehydrate the skin and tissues</li>
<li>dilate blood vessels, which shows up as redness</li>
<li>reduce sleep quality</li>
<li>interfere with nutrients your skin uses for repair, such as zinc and vitamin A, especially with heavy drinking</li>
</ul>
<p>The best data I know comes from the Nurses’ Health Study II, which followed 82,737 US women. White wine was linked with a 14% higher rosacea risk at 1 to 3 glasses a month and up to 49% higher at five or more glasses a week, liquor with an 8 to 28% higher risk depending on the amount, while beer and red wine were not significantly linked. It is an association, not proof of cause, and the researchers say more work is needed.</p>
<p>On skin I would watch for dullness, puffiness, flushing and rosacea flare-ups. If you have rosacea, a dermatologist can help you work out your own triggers.</p>

<h2>Dairy, for some people</h2>
<p>Research suggests dairy may affect hormonal signalling in some individuals. The idea is that dairy may increase IGF-1 signalling, IGF-1 can stimulate oil production, and extra sebum plus inflammation can lead to acne.</p>
<p>In a meta-analysis of 14 observational studies covering 78,529 children, teenagers and young adults, any dairy was linked with higher odds of acne (about 25% higher), with milk, yogurt and cheese all showing a link. The authors cautioned that the results are affected by differences between studies and publication bias, and the adjusted results were weaker than the unadjusted ones.</p>
<div class="callout"><div class="up">Important</div><p>Many people tolerate dairy perfectly, and dairy is also a useful source of protein, calcium and iodine. Skin response is very individual, so I would not cut dairy out “just in case”. If you want to test it, change one thing at a time and give it several weeks.</p></div>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/img/oat-drink-matcha.webp" alt="A carton of oat drink next to a tin of matcha, a bamboo whisk in a white bowl, a small strainer and a pink blender" style="max-height:none;object-fit:contain"><figcaption>A plant-based option: oat drink and matcha.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Sleep deprivation</h2>
<p>Your skin repairs itself mostly while you sleep. Too little sleep may lead to:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>reduced collagen production</li>
<li>increased cortisol</li>
<li>slower cell turnover</li>
<li>more inflammation</li>
</ul>
<p>In a small study of women, poor sleepers showed more signs of skin ageing and slower recovery of the skin barrier after stress to the skin than good sleepers. On skin I would watch for a dull complexion, dark circles and slower healing. This is why I treat sleep as part of my skincare.</p>

<h2>How I would find my own triggers</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Look for patterns, not villains.</strong> Notice when your skin flares: after poor sleep, a stressful week, a lot of sugar or alcohol.</li>
<li><strong>Change one thing at a time.</strong> Give it several weeks before you judge.</li>
<li><strong>Eat regularly.</strong> Protein, healthy fats and fibre keep blood sugar steadier.</li>
<li><strong>Protect sleep.</strong> It is the cheapest repair tool I know.</li>
<li><strong>Do not over-restrict.</strong> Cutting many foods at once makes it impossible to learn anything and can leave you under-fuelled.</li>
<li><strong>Keep skincare gentle.</strong> My <a href="/skin-guide/barrier-repair/">barrier repair playbook</a> shows how.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><div class="up">When I would see a doctor</div><p>If you have persistent or painful acne, rosacea, eczema, or sudden skin changes, please see a dermatologist. I am not a medical professional, and this note is not a substitute for one.</p></div>

